  "repeats": [],
  "notes": [
   "Parts changed from step 1: added wire7 (carrier VIN 12V to relay COM), because the relay must switch the 12V to the pump and step 1 had no wire to feed COM. The 'Next step' power image was not available, so this follows the text.",
   "The MKR Connector Carrier has no ports in the catalogue, so its edges use a null port. The Grove sockets are A0 (moisture, gcable1), D0 (water, gcable2), D1 (relay, gcable3) and TWI (I2C hub, gcable4). The VIN and GND screw terminals take wire3, wire4, wire7 and wire6.",
   "The MKR Zero is modelled as plugged into the carrier headers, so every MKR pin reaches the carrier. The MKR Zero is a 3.3 V board.",
   "Grove cable pin order is 1 = signal (SIG or SCL), 2 = second signal (NC or SDA), 3 = VCC, 4 = GND. The unused NC pin on the analog and digital modules is not given an electrical edge.",
   "The DPS310 and the OLED share the I2C bus through the I2C hub, which then goes to the carrier TWI port.",
   "The relay is driven from D1 (SIG). Its COM is fed from the 12V rail (carrier VIN) and NO goes to the pump. The pump return goes to GND.",
   "The water sensor hack is two solid wires of different lengths, soldered to two non-adjacent tinned bars. The sensor stays outside the tank and the wires go into the water. Wire ends toward the water are left free.",
   "The pump is a 12V pump, and the tutorial says an electronic valve could replace it. The vase and plant are omitted."
  ]
 },
